Q: How do you write your Springboot main class? A: <p>Wrapping <code>`SpringApplication.run()`</code> in a <code>`try-catch(Throwable)`</code> is generally considered an anti-pattern. Spring Boot is explicitly designed to handle bootstrapping errors (like malformed YAMLs) natively.</p>
<p>If your application is exiting silently on a YAML error, the root cause is usually a misconfigured logging framework (e.g., Logback initializing before the context and swallowing standard output), not the <code>`main`</code> method itself.</p>
<p>For an app with many committers where you want explicit, loud failures on startup without dirtying the <code>`main`</code> method, the idiomatic approach is to use an <code>`ApplicationListener`</code> listening for the <code>`ApplicationFailedEvent`</code>.</p>
<p>The Idiomatic Solution: <code>`ApplicationFailedEvent`</code></p>
<p>Leave your <code>`main`</code> class perfectly clean:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>
@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Register the listener directly before running
        SpringApplication app = new SpringApplication(DemoApplication.class);
        app.addListeners(new StartupFailureListener());
        app.run(args);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then, create a dedicated listener to handle the crash. This guarantees that no matter where the initialization fails, your custom logic will execute safely:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import org.springframework.boot.context.event.ApplicationFailedEvent;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationListener;

public class StartupFailureListener implements ApplicationListener&lt;ApplicationFailedEvent&gt; {

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ApplicationFailedEvent event) {
        System.err.println(&quot;========================================&quot;);
        System.err.println(&quot;!!! CRITICAL STARTUP FAILURE !!!&quot;);
        System.err.println(&quot;========================================&quot;);

        Throwable exception = event.getException();
        System.err.println(&quot;Reason: &quot; + exception.getMessage());

        // Optional: Print stack trace
        exception.printStackTrace(); 
    }
}
</code></pre>
